# apex patch: present a persona GPU to WebGPU (navigator.gpu)
#
# THE PROBLEM. WebGPU is the rising fingerprinting surface (detection-sota-2026:
# GPU-adapter probing is the dominant real-world WebGPU fingerprint use, and
# WebGPU-SPY reaches 90% site-level fingerprinting). navigator.gpu leaks the
# HOST GPU through five surfaces, none spoofed before this patch:
#
#   1. GPUAdapterInfo identity strings  -- adapter.info.{vendor, architecture,
#      device (hex device ID), description (device name), driver (backend
#      string)} report the real host GPU. A Linux/SwiftShader host that claims
#      a "MacBook Pro M1 Pro" persona via the UA and WebGL strings answers
#      WebGPU with the SwiftShader/virtual GPU -- an incoherence a detector can
#      score directly.
#   2. GPUAdapter.type / GPUAdapterInfo.adapterType  -- "cpu"/"integrated"/
#      "discrete" bus class, raw from the real backend.
#   3. adapter.limits / device.limits  -- a software adapter reports
#      spec-typical limits (identical across the whole fleet) instead of the
#      limits the claimed GPU class would have.
#   4. adapter.features / device.features  -- capability sets that don't match
#      the persona's claimed hardware.
#   5. device.adapterInfo  -- re-reads the adapter's identity (it inherits
#      whatever #1 says, so fixing #1 fixes this too).
#
# THE FIX. In GPUAdapter's constructor the five identity strings and the
# adapter/backend type are rewritten from the APEX_FP_WEBGPU_* persona env
# (emitted by stealth-browser fp_profiles.py, coherent with the profile's
# gpu_class and WebGL renderer string) AFTER the real values are read. The
# wgpu limits struct is overridden per-limit (APEX_FP_WEBGPU_LIMIT_<Name>)
# before it is copied into GPUSupportedLimits -- the same override is applied
# in GPUDevice::Initialize so device.limits agrees with adapter.limits (the
# clamp rule is conditional -- see LIMIT OVERRIDE CLAMP RULE below). The
# feature set is filtered to the APEX_FP_WEBGPU_FEATURES allowlist (subset
# only: a claimed feature the real adapter lacks would fail requestDevice
# validation) in both surfaces. The adapter/device handles stay real, so
# rendering behavior is unchanged; only the reported fingerprint is rewritten.
#
# LIMIT OVERRIDE CLAMP RULE. Each APEX_FP_WEBGPU_LIMIT_<Name> override is
# clamped to the real limit ONLY when the real adapter is actual hardware
# (discrete/integrated): the GPU process validates requests against the REAL
# limits, so an override above them just moves the rejection error into a
# named name (a host leak). When the real adapter is software (SwiftShader
# reports AdapterType::CPU -- VK_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_TYPE_CPU) or undetectable
# (Unknown), there is no real hardware to validate against, so the CLAIMED
# value is reported directly: a discrete persona's limits stay coherent with
# its claimed description instead of being clamped to the container's real
# SwiftShader limits (the incoherent "NVIDIA RTX 3060 + maxTextureDimension2D
# = 8192" pair this would otherwise produce). The real type is read from the
# real handle (apex_webgpu::RealAdapterType; on the adapter side the local
# `info` from GetInfo() is already available and is never spoofed).
#
# The default env has no APEX_FP_WEBGPU_* set -> every patch falls through to
# the real values (stock behavior).
#
# Not covered (deferred): the persistent pipeline-compile-state leak (compile
# times differ on a second origin because GPU-process state survived) -- it
# lives in the GPU process's Dawn cache and needs a partitioning change there;
# the probe (scripts/webgpu_probe_cdp.py) records the timing so the next build
# can measure it.
#
# Apply target: third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/{gpu_adapter.cc,
# gpu_device.cc} (anchor edits; helper: chromium_src/.../apex_webgpu.h)
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc
@@ GPUAdapter::GPUAdapter @@ (after the description_/driver_ assignments)
+  // apex-webgpu-identity: the persona GPU replaces the host GPU
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyStringOverride("APEX_FP_WEBGPU_VENDOR", &vendor_);
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyStringOverride("APEX_FP_WEBGPU_ARCH", &architecture_);
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyStringOverride("APEX_FP_WEBGPU_DEVICE_ID", &device_);
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyStringOverride("APEX_FP_WEBGPU_DESCRIPTION", &description_);
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyStringOverride("APEX_FP_WEBGPU_DRIVER", &driver_);
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyAdapterTypeOverride(&adapter_type_);
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyBackendTypeOverride(&backend_type_);
@@ GPUAdapter::GPUAdapter @@ (after features_ = MakeFeatureNameSet(...))
+  features_ = apex_webgpu::FilterFeaturesByAllowlist(features_);
@@ GPUAdapter::GPUAdapter @@ (before limits_ = MakeGarbageCollected<...>(limits))
+  // info.adapterType = the REAL type (never spoofed -- the spoof rewrites
+  // the adapter_type_ member, not the GetInfo() local).
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyLimitOverrides(&limits, info.adapterType);
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_device.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_device.cc
@@ GPUDevice::Initialize @@ (before limits_ = MakeGarbageCollected<...>(limits))
+  // adapter_'s REAL handle (the spoof rewrites identity, not the handle)
+  // supplies the real type for the same conditional clamp rule.
+  apex_webgpu::ApplyLimitOverrides(
+      &limits, apex_webgpu::RealAdapterType(adapter_->GetHandle()));
@@ GPUDevice::Initialize @@ (after features_ = MakeGarbageCollected<...>(features))
+  features_ = apex_webgpu::FilterFeaturesByAllowlist(features_);
